    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **Lawrence Butler**, whose 73.47 score against Javier makes him our premier start. **Butler** benefits from a massive 19% boost due to the favorable SP matchup and a dynamic platoon advantage, placing him in a prime position to capitalize on a high-ceiling outing. Similarly, **Jonathan Aranda** (70.47) is a high-conviction play against Baz; a +16% park factor and +12% platoon edge effectively negate the pitcher's talent profile. While **Dylan Beavers** (68.38) and **JJ Bleday** (66.17) round out our most potent offensive threats, pay close attention to **Bryce Harper** (64.81), who remains a must-start despite the moderate matchup against Leahy, as his elite xwOBA remains the backbone of our run-production stability.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench today involved **Jung Hoo Lee**, who narrowly missed the cut with a 48.89 score. While **Lee**\u2019s sprint speed profile is exactly the type of scarce asset we prioritize for SB gains, the combination of Bennett's efficiency and a negative platoon split for **Lee** pushed him just below the threshold required to displace **Gavin Sheets** or **Justin Crawford**. We are keeping **Daulton Varsho** and **Braden Montgomery** in reserve as well, as neither could overcome the superior daily efficiency scores generated by our starters in their respective matchups. \n\nRegarding risks, **Cal Raleigh** enters his matchup against Imanaga with a caution flag. While **Raleigh** holds a 56.88, the combination of a high-caliber pitcher and a park penalty makes him our most vulnerable starter; he occupies a slot only because the catcher market is thin. Additionally, while we are confident in the projected order, ensure you monitor the status of **Gleyber Torres** and **Zack Gelof**, as any late lineup shuffle in these spots could necessitate a quick pivot to our depth, though currently, their favorable park factors and SP matchups align to provide a strong floor for the LawDog roster today.",
